<h2>What would establish a binding production constraint?</h2>
<p>A component is binding within a disruption window if getting more of it would restore feasible deliveries, with other constraints held fixed. The test needs plant and platform data on planned output, parts per vehicle, current inventories, contracted allocations, compatible suppliers and the earliest time an alternative can ship. Track assembly loss and finished-vehicle import loss separately so a vehicle missing for both reasons is counted once. Compare the model with a documented disruption before reporting effects on real output or claiming that a given policy reduces them.</p>
<aside class="panel" aria-labelledby="revision">
  <h2 id="revision">Revision note · 24 November 2026</h2>
  <p>The previous overview used a single battery-value percentage without a matched cost or price denominator, and treated concentrated upstream stages as binding constraints. This revision uses separate output and import measures and documents the limits on replacing a part or a finished vehicle.</p>
</aside>
